Falsifies `system_tests::user::tests::a_process_composed_from_two_capabilities_runs_in_the_space_it_built`.

Architecture: aarch64, riscv64, x86_64

The defect: `ThreadControlBlock::START`, invoked from userspace, answers success and starts
nothing. Every step before it still works, so the witness reads its child out of the archive,
builds the space, is refused the double map, configures the thread and is told it started. Only
the thread never runs. This is the claim the test exists for and the one the witness carried
nowhere before §185 (what carries the claim that userspace composes a process from an authority
you can count on one hand): that a thread a two-capability process built runs in the space it
built. The kernel-side `start_thread_control_block` is untouched, so the harness still starts the
witness itself; only the witness's own `START` is broken.

Expected to fail: the deadline assertion, "the witness sent no verdict in 30 s: it is still
waiting for a child it started", because the witness is parked in its receive for an answer that
cannot come. Confirmed red on aarch64 on 2026-10-05 (UTC) with `cargo xtask test --arch aarch64
--test a_process_composed_from_two_capabilities`, at that assertion; the same command without the
patch passes. riscv64 and x86_64 are first replayed by the per-PR `--affected-since` sweep.

Nothing here is architecture-specific, so one patch serves every leg.

diff --git a/kernel/src/syscall.rs b/kernel/src/syscall.rs
--- a/kernel/src/syscall.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/syscall.rs
@@ -1472,7 +1472,7 @@ fn thread_control_block_invoke(
             if !rights.allows(Rights::WRITE) {
                 return Err(Error::NotPermitted);
             }
-            sched::start_thread_control_block(tid, [a0, a1, a2])?; // the child's x0, x1, x2 (19d/19e)
+            let _ = (tid, a0, a1, a2);
             Ok(0)
         }
         _ => Err(Error::BadMethod),
